// A precondition no i64 satisfies. The harness draws over the whole type here, so zero
// satisfying draws means the `requires` itself is unsatisfiable: a real defect, and a hard
// failure. This is the half of the anti-vacuity gate that must NOT become a skip when a
// constructed struct is in play (see contractTestsStructs.milo::counterDrained).
fn neverCallable(n: i64): i64
requires n > 0 && n < 0
ensures result == n
{
    return n
}
